iv. Reflection from Part A

I gained a better understanding of Agentic Software Engineering and how Artificial Intelligence is changing the way software systems are developed 
and used. Before exploring this topic, I mainly associated software applications with programs that follow instructions and produce results based on 
user inputs. However, I learned that AI agents can go beyond this by observing their environment, making decisions, and performing actions to achieve 
specific goals.

One thing that stood out to me is how AI agents differ from traditional software applications. Their ability to adapt and perform tasks with minimal 
human intervention opens up more possibilities for developing intelligent applications. I also realized that developing these systems requires continuous 
improvement. This is why Agile methodologies are important, as they allow developers to test, evaluate, and improve their systems through regular feedback.
Another important lesson I gained is that developing autonomous AI systems also comes with risks and responsibilities. Issues such as incorrect decisions, 
security threats, privacy violations, and unexpected behavior show that AI should not be given complete control without proper limitations and monitoring. 
Most importantly, I realized that human oversight remains essential even as AI becomes more advanced. AI can assist people in making decisions and 
completing tasks, but human judgment, responsibility, and accountability should still be considered.

To conclude, Part A helped me appreciate the connection between Software Engineering and Artificial Intelligence. As a Computer Engineering student, 
I am interested in learning how these technologies can be combined to develop practical solutions to real-world problems. I look forward to gaining 
more knowledge and hands-on experience in designing and developing intelligent software systems throughout this course.
